actionIds":[]},{"unitId":"consistency","phase":"chunk_review","automaticDispatches":2,"retriesUsed":1,"userDirectedDispatches":0,"diagnostics":[{"code":"invalid_output","summary":"Resolution requires accepted fresh inspection without mandatory gaps"}],"actionIds":[]}],"report":{"reviewId":"consolidated-chat-progress-s1-lld","attempt":2,"artifactId":"LLD-206db3426ffd4428-s1","status":"incomplete","counts":{"HIGH":0,"MED":3,"LOW":0},"chunks":[{"chunkId":"contracts","status":"changes_requested","summary":"The assigned scope, cardinalities, sibling boundaries, producer lifecycle operations and fencing rules align with the DEF and HLD. Grant reattachment and revocation are substantially specified, but initial authorization still depends on an undefined trusted-origin bootstrap.","findings":[{"id":"contracts-03","severity":"MED","location":"docs/epics/show-long-running-task-progress-chat-E20261109206db342/S001/LLD.md:123","message":"Define how an ordinary launching connection or project/conversation adapter first becomes authorized. The design requires an 'authorized launch', 'already verified origin' and 'trusted originating project adapter', while explicitly denying grant issuance to unbound ordinary IPC callers, but supplies no admission operation, evidence or verification rule that establishes those bindings. Current socket context establishes connection identity only; the step host's ordinary cwd/client inputs and TargetRegistry's label/PID routing do not prove conversation ownership. The new grants protect subsequent attachment, but cannot establish their own initial issuer authorization. Specify the daemon-owned bootstrap and its refusal behavior, including how a verified conversation/project adapter is distinguished from another ordinary connection, so s1/ac4 and downstream adapters have an implementable security boundary.","evidenceReferences":["docs/epics/show-long-running-task-progress-chat-E20261109206db342/S001/LLD.md:79-83","docs/epics/show-long-running-task